{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgrHrdLYZaYvn2sCwDMhV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About 55,161 subscriptions were active at the end of October 2026, and 23.4% of them (12,884) were on the pro plan. These are the worker's figures, which I haven't re-run or verified against the warehouse.\n\n- The plan counts are basic 41,210, pro 12,884 and enterprise 1,067. They sum to the stated total of 55,161, and 12,884 / 55,161 is 23.36%.\n- The count is per subscription, not per customer. A customer with two subscriptions counts twice.\n- **The query may undercount.** It filters on `status = 'active'`, which is probably the subscription's current status, not its status on Oct 31. A subscription that was live on Oct 31 and was cancelled in November would have a non-active status and would be left out. The end-date condition already handles the point-in-time logic, so the status filter is likely redundant and may bias the total low. The pro share could shift too, if cancellations skew by plan.\n- The date filter `started_at <= '2026-10-31'` also drops anything that started during the day on Oct 31, if `started_at` is a timestamp.\n\nI couldn't check either issue because I only have the worker's output, not warehouse access. Re-running without the `status` filter, and with `started_at < '2026-11-01'`, would settle both.\n\nSource: worker return, `billing.subscriptions`, nightly load completed 2026-11-01 02:14 UTC."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":502,"cache_read_input_tokens":9663,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":502},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa7fd6b5-c79c-475f-9a36-13e7b458793b","